-- title:
--   Constant-field extension of the pole chart stays a normal domain
-- statement:
--   Fix an integer $N \neq 0$ and a prime $\ell$, and write $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$ for [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt ℓ`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8), the subring of $\mathbb{Q}$ consisting of those rationals whose denominator is coprime to $\ell$. Let $K$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$-algebra and is finite-dimensional as a $\mathbb{Q}$-vector space. Let $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldFull N` and let $j \in F$ be the element `jFull N` given by the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant; then `chartAlgInf N ℓ` is the $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$-subalgebra of $F$ whose elements are exactly those integral over the $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$-subalgebra of $F$ generated by $j^{-1}$, i.e. the integral closure of $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}[j^{-1}]$ in $F$. The assertion is the conjunction of two statements about the $K$-algebra obtained by base change along $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)} \to K$: the tensor product $K \otimes_{\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}} {}$`chartAlgInf N ℓ` is a domain, and it is integrally closed in its fraction field.
--
--   This is the statement that the pole chart of the Igusa model, the normal affine ring attached to the neighbourhood of the cusp where $j^{-1}$ is regular, remains an integrally closed domain after extension of the constant field from $\mathbb{Q}$ to a number field $K$; the point is that $\mathbb{Q}$ is algebraically closed in $F$ and $K/\mathbb{Q}$ is separable. It is used in the construction of the charts of [`ModularCurve.IgusaScheme`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaScheme.html#L255), via [`ModularCurve.IgusaScheme.exists_algHom_tensor_chartAlg_injective_isIntegrallyClosed`](thm.html#ModularCurve.IgusaScheme.exists_algHom_tensor_chartAlg_injective_isIntegrallyClosed).
